<p>
Dynomight has looked at the health effects of vitamin D supplementation. The
large-scale meta-analyses that have been performed conclude there is no
significant effect, even though individual studies relatively consistently point
in the direction of an effect. This means our failure to detect a significant
effect may be due to low experiment power.
</p>



<p>
Dynomight suggests a mechanism for that: what if the low power of the existing
randomised trials is because they tend to be run in countries that fortify
common foods with vitamin D? That would mean, practically speaking, all arms of
the trial get the treatment, which certainly would make the experiment weaker.
</p>



<p>
To investigate, Dynomight pulls out a table with the results only from studies
where participants entered with relatively low values of vitamin D in their
blood.
